<div class="article-body">

<p>If you want the short answer on the best lettuce varieties for indoor growing: buy a looseleaf type first. Black Seeded Simpson or a Lollo Rossa, then one butterhead like Buttercrunch, and one mini romaine like Little Gem if you want crunch. Those three cover soft leaves, salad bulk, and sandwich lettuce, and all of them give you a second harvest from the same plant. Skip iceberg. It needs a long cool season and a patience you do not have on a kitchen shelf.</p>

<p>I did not start with that answer. I started with 8 packets, 41 dollars and 60 cents of seed, one 2 foot shelf, and a 40 watt bar light. Two plants per variety, 16 plants, same feed, same water, same room. Over 14 weeks I weighed every cutting on a $12 kitchen scale and wrote the number on the tray with a grease pencil.</p>

<p>The spread was stupid. The best variety gave me 214 grams per plant across three cuts. The worst gave me 61 grams and one cut before it turned bitter. Both cost under 4 dollars a packet. The difference was not price, and it was not the brand on the label. It was whether the plant regrows from the center or gives you one head and quits. That is the whole game indoors.</p>

<h2 id="types">1. The 3 Groups That Matter on a Shelf</h2>

<p>Lettuce catalogs split into crisphead, butterhead, romaine, looseleaf, and stem. For an indoor shelf, that list is too long by two. Here is what actually changes your outcome.</p>

