<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> indoors, the trait that pays your light bill is regrowth, not flavor. You are running a lamp 16 hours a day whether the plant gives you one head or three cuttings. A 45 day looseleaf that yields three times beats a 75 day iceberg that yields once. The iceberg may weigh more per head, but you cannot run it at 75F in a spare room without it refusing to head at all.</p>

<p>Iceberg is the one variety group I would tell a beginner to skip outright. It is bred for a long, cool, bright field season. On a shelf it either stays loose and floppy or it starts to bolt from the middle, and either way you spent 11 weeks of electricity on a plant that looks like a weed. Every grower thread about "my lettuce never forms a head indoors" is usually this.</p>

<p>One more thing about labels. "Butterhead" and "romaine" and "looseleaf" are categories, not varieties. A listing that says "Butterhead Lettuce Seeds" and nothing else is telling you almost nothing. You want the variety name: Buttercrunch, Little Gem, Salanova, Nevada, Yugoslavian Red. No name means no way to compare it against anything.</p>

