<p><strong>Pay attention to this number:</strong> 42 days. That is Black Seeded Simpson to a cuttable plant in a 68F room. It is also the number that makes indoor lettuce worth doing. At 42 days you can run two trays staggered two weeks apart, and cut salad every 14 days forever, from about 4 square feet of shelf.</p>

<p>Nevada surprised me. Batavia types are not the ones people name in indoor threads, and it came in with the densest, crispest leaves of the loose types. If you like texture more than tenderness, that is the packet.</p>

<p>Little Gem earned its reputation for flavor and lost on regrowth. First cut was 96 grams of proper crunchy romaine. Second cut was 31. Third was not worth weighing. It is a plant you grow for one good head, so treat it as a one-shot crop and stagger more of them instead of expecting it to come back.</p>

<h2 id="picks">3. Which Packet to Order for Your Setup</h2>

<p>Match the packet to the space and to how you eat, not to a top ten list.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>One tray, maximum salad per square foot:</strong> <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Black+Seeded+Simpson+lettuce+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Black Seeded Simpson lettuce seeds</a>. About 3 to 4 dollars for a packet with several hundred seeds. This is the variety I would hand a first-timer with no notes.</li>
  <li><strong>You want the plate to look expensive:</strong> <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Lollo+Rossa+lettuce+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Lollo Rossa lettuce seeds</a>, the frilly red one. Same growth speed as the greens, and it is the only variety my girlfriend has ever commented on.</li>
  <li><strong>Sandwiches and wraps:</strong> <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Little+Gem+lettuce+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Little Gem lettuce seeds</a>. Small upright heads, crisp heart, 58 days. Plant more of them than you think, because it does not come back strong.</li>
  <li><strong>A warm room, 75F and up:</strong> <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Nevada+batavia+lettuce+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Nevada batavia lettuce seeds</a>. Bred for heat, and it held up two weeks longer than the butterheads before it went bitter.</li>
  <li><strong>Soft leaves for burgers and tacos:</strong> Buttercrunch. Pick the outer leaves and the center keeps producing. Do not cut the whole head.</li>
</ul>

<p>You do not need a special system. My whole build is a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=seed+starting+tray+with+humidity+dome&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">seed starting tray with a humidity dome</a> for germination, then a shallow tray of clay pebbles and a weak nutrient solution. For light, one <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Barrina+T5+LED+grow+light+bar+2ft&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Barrina T5 LED bar</a> about 6 inches above the canopy. The full setup walkthrough, including the nutrient ratio that stops tip burn, is in the <a href="/posts/grow-lettuce-indoors-year-round">year round indoor lettuce guide</a>. If you are running a stronger panel, check the <a href="/posts/grow-light-distance-chart">grow light distance chart</a> before you hang it, because lettuce bleaches at half the distance a tomato wants. If you are comparing bars by price and measured output instead of box wattage, the <a href="/grow-lights">grow light database</a> has the numbers for the popular models.</p>

<h2 id="bolt">4. Why the "Bolt Resistant" Label Failed at Day 38</h2>

<p>I thought I had this figured out. Four of my eight packets said bolt resistant, and I built the whole schedule around that word.</p>

<p>Then the Buttercrunch tray went bitter at day 38, twelve days before its stated maturity, in a room that never got above 73F. The Yugoslavian Red next to it, a variety with no heat claim at all, kept going to day 57. That is backwards from what the packets promised.</p>

<p><strong>This is where it gets interesting.</strong> Bolt resistance in lettuce is mostly a field trait. It is bred against summer heat and long days in open ground, where the plant is fighting 90F afternoons. Indoors I had two different stressors that the label was never written for. One was a reservoir that drifted to 78F, because the pump sat next to the light driver. The other was an 18 hour photoperiod I had set because "more light equals more growth."</p>

<p>Lettuce is a long day plant. Stretch the day past about 16 hours and you are not feeding it more, you are telling it the season is ending. Hydroponic growers hit this constantly: the thread titled "my lettuce bolts at 40 to 45 days every time" is the same story with a different room. The plant is not sick. It has been told to flower.</p>

<p><strong>Here is why that matters:</strong> the fix cost me nothing and it beat every "heat tolerant" packet on the shelf. I dropped the timer to 14 hours, moved the pump off the light driver shelf so the water sat at 68F instead of 78F, and kept the same seed. The next Buttercrunch run went 61 days before it bolted. Same packet. Same lamp. Two changes in the environment, no change in the variety.</p>

<p>So use bolt resistance as a tiebreaker, not a plan. If your room runs warm, buy Nevada or Oakleaf. If your room runs cool and you control the timer, any of the eight work, and the cheap one works as well as the expensive one.</p>

<h2 id="math">5. How Many Plants, and What a Salad Really Costs</h2>

<p>A packet is not a plant. Lettuce seed is small and cheap: a standard packet holds several hundred seeds, and my 3 dollar Black Seeded Simpson packet had well over 600. At two plants per week you are looking at years of seed from one envelope, which is why the "$4 packet" panic in some guides is misplaced.</p>

<p>The number that actually matters is grams per square foot per week. My best tray, 6 plants of Black Seeded Simpson in a 12 by 24 inch tray, gave 214 grams per plant over three cuts. That is about 1.3 kilos of leaf from 2 square feet over 14 weeks, or roughly 90 grams a week, which is a large salad every other day.</p>

<p>Compare that to the bag at the store. A 5 ounce clamshell of organic spring mix is 142 grams and runs 4 to 5 dollars here. The leaf I grew came to about 5 and a half pounds across all 16 plants in 14 weeks, which is roughly 17 of those clamshells, or 70 to 85 dollars at store prices. My seed cost 41 dollars and 60 cents, the light was 40, and the electricity for the run was about 9, so the first 14 weeks land close to a wash. Here is why I still call it cheap: the second run costs almost nothing, because the light is bought and one packet still holds a few hundred more seeds.</p>

<p>For a household of two, 6 plants in rotation is enough. For one person, 3. Do not plant a whole tray at once. Stagger a new cell block every 10 to 14 days so you are cutting one group while the next is filling in. The <a href="/posts/hydroponic-lettuce-not-growing">hydroponic lettuce troubleshooting guide</a> covers the stall that happens when you skip this and crowd a full tray of the same age.</p>

<h2 id="buy">6. The Buying Order That Saves You Money</h2>

<p>Three ways this goes, and I paid for the first one.</p>

<p><strong>The trap.</strong> The 12 dollar "indoor lettuce variety pack" with six unnamed seed tapes. Half germinated, none of them came back after cutting, and I still do not know what I grew. Cheap per seed, expensive per salad, and useless as a lesson because there was no variety name to learn from.</p>

<p><strong>The overkill.</strong> The full Salanova pelleted seed program with a precision seeder, one-cut harvesting, and 300 plants. It is genuinely how commercial growers hit uniform heads, and it is a real system. You are growing salad for two people on a shelf, and a one-cut variety means you throw the root ball away every time.</p>

<p><strong>The middle ground I run.</strong> Two packets, about 7 dollars total, and one 10 dollar tray.</p>

<ul>
  <li>One <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Black+Seeded+Simpson+lettuce+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">looseleaf lettuce seed packet</a> for volume and speed.</li>
  <li>One butterhead or mini romaine packet for texture, whatever looks good to you.</li>
  <li>One shallow tray with drainage, and one bar light if you do not already own one.</li>
</ul>

<p>That is the whole order. The seed is the cheap part and the only part where the variety name changes your result.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth about my top pick. Black Seeded Simpson is a boring green leaf with almost no flavor of its own. It is crisp, it is fast, it comes back three times, and it tastes like the idea of lettuce rather than lettuce. If you want a salad you actually look forward to, the Lollo Rossa and the Yugoslavian Red carry the flavor, and they yield less. My shelf runs both, because a 214 gram plant that tastes like nothing is a worse dinner than a 128 gram plant that tastes like something.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> start with one looseleaf packet and one butterhead, around 7 dollars for both, and put the money you saved into a timer instead of more seed. Set it to 14 hours and keep the reservoir under 70F. A 3 to 5 dollar packet is normal and anything over 8 dollars for a standard lettuce variety is a bad deal, because lettuce seed is the cheapest seed in the catalog. I burned 41 dollars and 60 cents across 8 packets to learn which three were worth replanting. Let my wallet take the hit instead of yours, and start with the 3 dollar one.</p>

